1
00:00:03,760 --> 00:00:06,130
So two years later, there's a knock on the door.

2
00:00:06,200 --> 00:00:08,300
Guy opens it, and there on his porch is the snail,

3
00:00:08,360 --> 00:00:10,360
who says, "What the heck was all that about?"

4
00:00:13,760 --> 00:00:15,660
I don't really get it.

5
00:00:15,730 --> 00:00:19,000
Well, see, it took two years for the snail to...

6
00:00:22,860 --> 00:00:25,300
Not important.

7
00:00:25,300 --> 00:00:28,000
- Can I ask you a question?  - Sure.

8
00:00:28,000 --> 00:00:29,860
Where do you think this is going?

9
00:00:31,300 --> 00:00:34,260
To be honest, I was hoping at least second base.

10
00:00:35,660 --> 00:00:37,260
You're so funny.

11
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:40,700
You're like a stand-up comedian.

12
00:00:40,700 --> 00:00:43,730
A Jewish stand-up comedian, that'd be new.

13
00:00:44,630 --> 00:00:48,760
Actually, I think a lot of them are Jewish.

14
00:00:48,760 --> 00:00:52,800
No, I was just... Never mind.

15
00:00:52,800 --> 00:00:55,630
Look, Howard, this is our third date

16
00:00:55,630 --> 00:00:57,660
and we both know what that means.

17
00:00:57,660 --> 00:01:00,630
- We do?  - Sex.

18
00:01:02,830 --> 00:01:04,800
You're kidding.

19
00:01:04,800 --> 00:01:07,500
But I need to know whether you're looking

20
00:01:07,500 --> 00:01:10,360
for a relationship or a one-night stand.

21
00:01:10,360 --> 00:01:12,200
Okay, just to be clear:

22
00:01:12,200 --> 00:01:15,200
there's only one correct answer, right?

23
00:01:15,200 --> 00:01:18,000
It's not like "chicken or fish" on an airplane?

24
00:01:18,000 --> 00:01:20,230
Maybe you need to think about it a little.

25
00:01:20,230 --> 00:01:22,360
You know, it's not unheard of

26
00:01:22,360 --> 00:01:25,500
for a one-night stand to turn into a relationship.

27
00:01:28,200 --> 00:01:30,830
Call me when you figure it out.

28
00:01:33,100 --> 00:01:36,630
Three dates means sex?

29
00:01:40,360 --> 00:01:42,760
Who knew?

30
00:01:45,360 --> 00:01:47,230
Greetings, homies.

31
00:01:48,160 --> 00:01:49,460
Homette.

32
00:01:49,460 --> 00:01:52,000
Why are you back from your date so early?

33
00:01:52,000 --> 00:01:54,800
In romance, as in show business,

34
00:01:54,800 --> 00:01:57,330
always leave them wanting more.

35
00:01:58,660 --> 00:02:01,030
What exactly does that mean?

36
00:02:01,030 --> 00:02:03,060
He struck out.

37
00:02:28,960 --> 00:02:30,760
Hey, did either of you guys know

38
00:02:30,760 --> 00:02:32,400
that three dates with the same woman

39
00:02:32,400 --> 00:02:34,300
is the threshold for sex?

40
00:02:34,300 --> 00:02:37,400
Actually, I've never had three dates with the same woman.

41
00:02:38,230 --> 00:02:40,930
With Penny and me, it took two years.

42
00:02:40,930 --> 00:02:44,800
Now that I think about it, that was three dates.

43
00:02:44,800 --> 00:02:47,600
Okay, well, before you and Penny hooked up,

44
00:02:47,600 --> 00:02:50,060
Did she ask for any kind of commitment?

45
00:02:50,060 --> 00:02:52,200
No, she was pretty clear about

46
00:02:52,200 --> 00:02:55,000
wanting to keep her options open.

47
00:02:55,000 --> 00:02:57,330
I have something to announce,

48
00:02:57,330 --> 00:02:59,800
but out of respect for convention, I will wait for you

49
00:02:59,800 --> 00:03:01,600
to finish your current conversation.

50
00:03:01,600 --> 00:03:03,260
What are you talking about?

51
00:03:03,260 --> 00:03:05,460
The cultural paradigm in which people have sex

52
00:03:05,460 --> 00:03:06,760
after three dates.

53
00:03:06,760 --> 00:03:08,100
I see.

54
00:03:08,100 --> 00:03:10,300
Now, are we talking "date," the social interaction,

55
00:03:10,300 --> 00:03:12,760
or "date," the dried fruit?

56
00:03:14,930 --> 00:03:17,530
Never mind, what's your announcement?

57
00:03:17,530 --> 00:03:20,000
Oh, good, my turn. Well, this is very exciting

58
00:03:20,000 --> 00:03:22,060
and I wanted you to be among the first to know...

59
00:03:22,060 --> 00:03:23,200
Hey, Cooper.

60
00:03:23,200 --> 00:03:24,600
I hear you're going to be on the radio

61
00:03:24,600 --> 00:03:26,230
with Ira Flatow from Science Friday next week.

62
00:03:26,230 --> 00:03:29,860
Thank you, Kripke, for depriving me of the opportunity

63
00:03:29,860 --> 00:03:32,100
to share my news with my friends.

64
00:03:32,100 --> 00:03:34,030
My pleasure.

65
00:03:34,030 --> 00:03:35,860
My "thank you" was not sincere.

66
00:03:35,860 --> 00:03:38,300
But "my pleasure" is.

67
00:03:38,300 --> 00:03:40,350
Let me ask you a question:

68
00:03:40,350 --> 00:03:43,330
At what point did National Public Radio

69
00:03:43,330 --> 00:03:45,260
have to start scraping the bottom of the barrel

70
00:03:45,260 --> 00:03:46,930
for its guests?

71
00:03:46,930 --> 00:03:49,760
Don't answer. It's rhetorical.

72
00:03:49,760 --> 00:03:52,900
Why are you such a stupid head?

73
00:03:53,660 --> 00:03:56,630
That is also rhetorical.

74
00:03:57,660 --> 00:03:59,500
I'm sorry you had to hear that.

75
00:03:59,500 --> 00:04:01,600
Are you really going to be on NPR?

76
00:04:01,600 --> 00:04:04,530
Yes, they're interviewing me by phone from my office,

77
00:04:04,530 --> 00:04:06,560
regarding the recent so-called "discovery"

78
00:04:06,560 --> 00:04:08,930
of magnetic monopoles in spin-ices.

79
00:04:08,930 --> 00:04:11,700
It's pledge week and they're trying to goose the ratings

80
00:04:11,700 --> 00:04:13,460
with a little controversy.

81
00:04:14,260 --> 00:04:15,900
Very cool, congratulations.

82
00:04:15,900 --> 00:04:18,130
Thank you. My mother is very excited.

83
00:04:18,130 --> 00:04:21,030
She's convening her Bible study group to listen in,

84
00:04:21,030 --> 00:04:23,500
and then pray for my soul.

85
00:04:24,430 --> 00:04:25,830
I was on the radio once.

86
00:04:25,830 --> 00:04:28,130
I called in to Fever 104 FM New Delhi

87
00:04:28,130 --> 00:04:30,560
and was the fourth person to say the phrase that pays:

88
00:04:30,560 --> 00:04:34,260
"Fever 104, aajke naye  acchha sangeetka ghar."

89
00:04:35,900 --> 00:04:38,700
That means: "Fever 104,

90
00:04:38,700 --> 00:04:41,230
home of the really good current music."

91
00:04:43,900 --> 00:04:45,600
It's much catchier in Hindi.

92
00:04:47,760 --> 00:04:51,200
All right. These are the talking points

93
00:04:51,200 --> 00:04:53,830
for my NPR interview tomorrow.

94
00:04:53,830 --> 00:04:55,830
I need to make sure that they're simple enough

95
00:04:55,830 --> 00:04:59,060
for the less educated in the audience to understand.

96
00:04:59,060 --> 00:05:02,700
Howard, look this over and tell me what's unclear to you.

97
00:05:02,700 --> 00:05:05,300
Excuse me, I have a master's degree in engineering

98
00:05:05,300 --> 00:05:07,400
from the Massachusetts Institute of Technology.

99
00:05:07,400 --> 00:05:10,100
It required the completion of 144 units of grad work

100
00:05:10,100 --> 00:05:11,530
and an original thesis.

101
00:05:11,530 --> 00:05:14,130
Yes.

102
00:05:14,130 --> 00:05:16,660
Look this over and tell me what's unclear to you.

103
00:05:18,600 --> 00:05:20,360
You know, when Sheldon gives you homework,

104
00:05:20,360 --> 00:05:22,260
you don't have to do it.

105
00:05:22,260 --> 00:05:23,960
In fact, it's better if you don't,

106
00:05:23,960 --> 00:05:26,200
otherwise it makes the rest of us look bad.

107
00:05:27,400 --> 00:05:29,800
- Hi, guys. - Hey. Hello.

108
00:05:29,800 --> 00:05:31,300
Yo, Raj, talk to me.

109
00:05:39,460 --> 00:05:41,330
I'm sorry, just screwing with you.

110
00:05:44,000 --> 00:05:46,630
Hey, Howard, why haven't you called Bernadette?

111
00:05:46,630 --> 00:05:48,130
Did she say something?

112
00:05:48,130 --> 00:05:49,830
Yeah, she said she hasn't heard from you in a week.

113
00:05:49,830 --> 00:05:51,030
I thought you liked her?

114
00:05:51,030 --> 00:05:53,430
I do, but she wants a commitment

115
00:05:53,430 --> 00:05:55,830
and I'm not sure she's my type.

116
00:05:55,830 --> 00:05:57,460
She agreed to go out with you for free.

117
00:05:57,460 --> 00:05:59,130
What more do you need?

118
00:05:59,130 --> 00:06:01,530
Look, Bernadette is really nice.

119
00:06:01,530 --> 00:06:03,800
I just always thought when I finally settle down

120
00:06:03,800 --> 00:06:05,960
into a relationship, it would be with someone,

121
00:06:05,960 --> 00:06:07,730
you know, different.

122
00:06:07,730 --> 00:06:09,230
Different how?

123
00:06:09,230 --> 00:06:13,430
Well, you know, more like Megan Fox from Transformers.

124
00:06:14,200 --> 00:06:15,500
Or Katee Sackhoff

125
00:06:15,500 --> 00:06:17,430
from Battlestar Galactica.

126
00:06:17,430 --> 00:06:19,830
Are you high?

127
00:06:20,830 --> 00:06:22,400
You'd have a better shot with the three-breasted

128
00:06:22,400 --> 00:06:24,900
Martian hooker from Total Recall.

129
00:06:26,130 --> 00:06:28,830
Okay, now you're just being unrealistic.

130
00:06:29,730 --> 00:06:31,360
Anyway, that movie was like 20 years ago.

131
00:06:31,360 --> 00:06:34,030
Imagine how saggy those thing would be.

132
00:06:37,360 --> 00:06:40,500
Howard, you're going to throw away a great girl like Bernadette

133
00:06:40,500 --> 00:06:43,160
because you're holding out for some ridiculous fantasy?

134
00:06:43,160 --> 00:06:47,100
Hey, just because you settled doesn't mean I have to.

135
00:06:48,500 --> 00:06:50,930
Excuse me, I'm sitting here.

136
00:06:51,730 --> 00:06:54,000
Hey, I did not settle for Leonard.

137
00:06:54,000 --> 00:06:58,200
I mean, obviously, he isn't the kind of guy I usually go out with...

138
00:06:58,200 --> 00:07:00,600
you know, physically.

139
00:07:00,600 --> 00:07:02,900
Again, I'm right here.

140
00:07:03,860 --> 00:07:06,000
My point is, I do not judge a book by its cover.

141
00:07:06,000 --> 00:07:07,660
I am interested in the person underneath.

142
00:07:07,660 --> 00:07:10,060
I am here, right? You see me.

143
00:07:15,260 --> 00:07:19,430
Hey, I'm interested in what's inside people, too,

144
00:07:19,430 --> 00:07:22,660
but why is it wrong to want those insides wrapped up in,

145
00:07:22,660 --> 00:07:26,330
say, the delicious caramel that is Halle Berry?

146
00:07:27,500 --> 00:07:30,760
Yes, you're delicious caramel, too.

147
00:07:36,030 --> 00:07:37,360
All right, you know what, I will tell you

148
00:07:37,360 --> 00:07:39,000
why it's wrong... Excuse me, may I interject?

149
00:07:39,000 --> 00:07:40,000
What?

150
00:07:40,000 --> 00:07:41,860
Biologically speaking,

151
00:07:41,860 --> 00:07:44,900
Howard is perfectly justified in seeking out the optimum mate

152
00:07:44,900 --> 00:07:47,560
for the propagation of his genetic line.

153
00:07:47,560 --> 00:07:49,760
- Thank you, Sheldon. - Now, whether that

154
00:07:49,760 --> 00:07:51,930
propagation is in the interest of humanity is, of course,

155
00:07:51,930 --> 00:07:53,760
an entirely different question.

156
00:07:55,960 --> 00:07:58,300
This is Ira Flatow and you're listening

157
00:07:58,300 --> 00:08:01,230
to NPR's Science Friday.

158
00:08:01,230 --> 00:08:02,700
Joining us today by phone

159
00:08:02,700 --> 00:08:05,200
from his office in Pasadena, California

160
00:08:05,200 --> 00:08:07,230
is Dr. Sheldon Cooper.

161
00:08:07,230 --> 00:08:09,960
Oh, this is going to be a riot.

162
00:08:12,460 --> 00:08:14,500
Thanks for being with us today, Dr. Cooper.

163
00:08:14,500 --> 00:08:17,160
My pleasure, Ira.

164
00:08:17,160 --> 00:08:20,130
Now, let's talk about magnetic monopoles.

165
00:08:20,130 --> 00:08:24,160
Can you explain to our audience just what a monopole is?

166
00:08:24,160 --> 00:08:25,330
Of course.

167
00:08:25,330 --> 00:08:29,730
First, consider an ordinary magnet which has,

168
00:08:29,730 --> 00:08:31,360
as even the most uneducated

169
00:08:31,360 --> 00:08:34,130
in your audience must know, two poles.

170
00:08:36,060 --> 00:08:39,230
A north and south pole.

171
00:08:39,230 --> 00:08:42,730
If you cut that in half, you have two smaller magnets,

172
00:08:42,730 --> 00:08:45,460
each with its own north and south pole.

173
00:08:45,460 --> 00:08:48,460
Uh, Dr. Cooper, I think there might be

174
00:08:48,460 --> 00:08:49,960
something wrong with our connection.

175
00:08:49,960 --> 00:08:53,330
No, I hear you fine.

176
00:08:53,330 --> 00:08:57,460
As I was saying, an ordinary magnet has two poles.

177
00:08:57,460 --> 00:09:00,030
The primary characteristic of a monopole

178
00:09:00,030 --> 00:09:03,600
is that it has only one pole, hence, monopole.

179
00:09:05,130 --> 00:09:07,230
A requirement for string theory,

180
00:09:07,230 --> 00:09:09,160
or M-theory, if you will,

181
00:09:09,160 --> 00:09:11,960
is the existence of such monopoles.

182
00:09:11,960 --> 00:09:14,200
I, myself, led an expedition

183
00:09:14,200 --> 00:09:16,300
to the Arctic Circle in search of said particles.

184
00:09:16,300 --> 00:09:18,930
Kripke, I found the nozzle! I'm going to kill you!

185
00:09:26,500 --> 00:09:28,600
So nice you could join me this evening.

186
00:09:28,600 --> 00:09:30,800
You're looking lovely as always.

187
00:09:32,260 --> 00:09:33,530
Thanks, Howard.

188
00:09:33,530 --> 00:09:36,330
Always nice to be part of your masturbatory fantasies.

189
00:09:38,430 --> 00:09:39,660
Come on, Katee.

190
00:09:39,660 --> 00:09:41,360
Don't make it sound so cheap.

191
00:09:41,360 --> 00:09:43,800
I'm sorry, fiddling with yourself in the bathtub

192
00:09:43,800 --> 00:09:45,660
is a real class act.

193
00:09:47,760 --> 00:09:49,360
Thank you.

194
00:09:49,360 --> 00:09:52,230
So, shall we get started?

195
00:09:53,330 --> 00:09:55,200
Sure.

196
00:09:55,200 --> 00:09:57,060
But can I ask you a question first?

197
00:09:57,060 --> 00:09:59,500
Do you want to play Cylon and colonist?

198
00:09:59,500 --> 00:10:00,860
No.

199
00:10:00,860 --> 00:10:02,700
I want to know why you're playing make-believe with me

200
00:10:02,700 --> 00:10:04,600
when you could be out with a real woman tonight.

201
00:10:04,600 --> 00:10:06,700
You mean, Bernadette?

202
00:10:06,700 --> 00:10:08,900
No, I mean Princess Leia.

203
00:10:08,900 --> 00:10:10,530
Of course I mean Bernadette.

204
00:10:10,530 --> 00:10:13,700
She's a wonderful girl and she really likes you.

205
00:10:13,700 --> 00:10:16,500
I know, but she's not you.

206
00:10:16,500 --> 00:10:18,800
I'm not me.

207
00:10:19,730 --> 00:10:20,830
The real me is in Beverly Hills

208
00:10:20,830 --> 00:10:22,530
Going out with a tall, handsome, rich guy.

209
00:10:22,530 --> 00:10:23,800
Really? Tall?

210
00:10:23,800 --> 00:10:26,460
- Six-four. - Ouch.

211
00:10:26,460 --> 00:10:29,100
The point is, you've got a wonderful girl in your life,

212
00:10:29,100 --> 00:10:31,030
And you're ignoring her in order to spend your nights

213
00:10:31,030 --> 00:10:34,230
in the bathtub with a mental image and a wash cloth.

214
00:10:36,630 --> 00:10:38,300
Howard!

215
00:10:38,300 --> 00:10:40,530
What are you doing in there?

216
00:10:40,530 --> 00:10:42,460
I'm taking a bath!

217
00:10:42,460 --> 00:10:44,530
I hope that's all you're doing!

218
00:10:44,530 --> 00:10:46,660
We share that tub!

219
00:10:49,000 --> 00:10:51,200
Don't remind me!

220
00:10:53,860 --> 00:10:55,560
Oh, man.

221
00:10:55,560 --> 00:10:59,300
All soaped up and no place to go.

222
00:11:07,100 --> 00:11:08,430
How ya doin', buddy?

223
00:11:08,430 --> 00:11:11,000
I was humiliated on national radio.

224
00:11:11,000 --> 00:11:12,160
How do you think I'm doing?

225
00:11:12,160 --> 00:11:13,960
Come on, it wasn't that bad.

226
00:11:13,960 --> 00:11:15,700
What do you want?

227
00:11:15,700 --> 00:11:17,860
(munchkin voice): We represent the Lollipop Guild,

228
00:11:17,860 --> 00:11:20,030
and we want you.

229
00:11:24,660 --> 00:11:27,800
Okay, so Kripke played a joke on you.

230
00:11:27,800 --> 00:11:29,100
It wasn't funny.

231
00:11:29,100 --> 00:11:31,160
- I thought it was funny. - Raj.

232
00:11:31,160 --> 00:11:32,500
You laughed.

233
00:11:33,960 --> 00:11:35,330
Did you laugh?

234
00:11:36,730 --> 00:11:38,730
I fell on the floor.

235
00:11:40,030 --> 00:11:41,700
All right, he got you.

236
00:11:41,700 --> 00:11:43,100
You can get him back.

237
00:11:43,100 --> 00:11:45,330
I refuse to sink to his level.

238
00:11:45,330 --> 00:11:47,500
You can't sink. With all that helium in you,

239
00:11:47,500 --> 00:11:49,700
you're lucky you don't float away.

240
00:11:52,860 --> 00:11:54,830
Are you really admitting defeat?

241
00:11:54,830 --> 00:11:56,300
I never admit defeat.

242
00:11:56,300 --> 00:11:57,460
Good.

243
00:11:57,460 --> 00:11:58,700
However, on an unrelated topic,

244
00:11:58,700 --> 00:12:01,630
I am never getting out of this bed again.

245
00:12:01,630 --> 00:12:03,730
What if you could make Kripke

246
00:12:03,730 --> 00:12:05,630
look even sillier than he made you look?

247
00:12:05,630 --> 00:12:06,800
I don't think that's possible, dude.

248
00:12:06,800 --> 00:12:08,230
You're not helping.

249
00:12:08,230 --> 00:12:11,300
I didn't come to help, I came to mock.

250
00:12:12,530 --> 00:12:14,900
Sheldon, what you need to do is figure out a way

251
00:12:14,900 --> 00:12:17,730
to exact vengeance on Kripke, like, uh,

252
00:12:17,730 --> 00:12:21,200
like, how the Joker got back at Batman for putting him

253
00:12:21,200 --> 00:12:24,200
in the Arkham Asylum for the Criminally Insane.

254
00:12:24,200 --> 00:12:25,900
That's true. He didn't just stay there and talk about

255
00:12:25,900 --> 00:12:27,300
His feelings with the other psychotic villains.

256
00:12:27,300 --> 00:12:30,730
He, he broke out and poisoned Gotham's water supply.

257
00:12:32,800 --> 00:12:34,600
Well...

258
00:12:34,600 --> 00:12:36,760
- I suppose I could poison Kripke. - No!

259
00:12:36,760 --> 00:12:38,230
No. It's a simple matter.

260
00:12:38,230 --> 00:12:39,760
There are several toxic chemicals

261
00:12:39,760 --> 00:12:42,760
that'd be untraceable in an autopsy.

262
00:12:43,760 --> 00:12:46,160
Okay, uh, that's the spirit,

263
00:12:46,160 --> 00:12:48,530
but, um, let's dial it back

264
00:12:48,530 --> 00:12:51,660
to a non-lethal form of vengeance.

265
00:12:54,030 --> 00:12:57,130
How about we whoopee cushion on his office chair?

266
00:12:58,260 --> 00:12:59,500
He'll sit down, it'll sound like

267
00:12:59,500 --> 00:13:01,960
he's flatulent, even though he's not.

268
00:13:03,800 --> 00:13:05,430
Let's keep thinking.

269
00:13:09,900 --> 00:13:11,730
Oh, gee, you're too late.

270
00:13:11,730 --> 00:13:14,100
Scarlett Johansson and Wonder Woman were just in here

271
00:13:14,100 --> 00:13:16,960
trolling around for neurotic, little weasels.

272
00:13:16,960 --> 00:13:19,830
I came to talk to Bernadette. She's working today, right?

273
00:13:19,830 --> 00:13:22,330
Yes, but I don't think she wants to see you.

274
00:13:22,330 --> 00:13:23,360
Why not?

275
00:13:23,360 --> 00:13:25,060
Come on, Howard. You hurt her feelings

276
00:13:25,060 --> 00:13:26,500
by not calling her all week.

277
00:13:26,500 --> 00:13:29,900
Plus, I've kind of been talkin' some smack about ya.

278
00:13:30,730 --> 00:13:31,900
Hello, Howard.

279
00:13:31,900 --> 00:13:34,030
Wait, Bernadette, I need to talk to you.

280
00:13:34,030 --> 00:13:35,160
I can't now, I'm working.

281
00:13:35,160 --> 00:13:36,700
This will only take a second.

282
00:13:36,700 --> 00:13:38,460
You asked me to think about where

283
00:13:38,460 --> 00:13:41,430
our relationship was going, and I did.

284
00:13:42,360 --> 00:13:44,200
Bernadette?

285
00:13:48,030 --> 00:13:50,500
Will you marry me?

286
00:13:52,200 --> 00:13:55,530
Is this more comedy that I don't understand?

287
00:13:55,530 --> 00:13:58,500
No. I'm serious.

288
00:13:58,500 --> 00:14:01,100
I'm never going to find another girl like you

289
00:14:01,100 --> 00:14:05,000
who likes me and is, you know, real.

290
00:14:07,030 --> 00:14:08,430
So, this isn't a joke?

291
00:14:08,430 --> 00:14:11,160
- No. - Then you're insane.

292
00:14:12,360 --> 00:14:15,830
I prefer to think of myself as quirky.

293
00:14:15,830 --> 00:14:18,200
Howard, we've only been on three dates.

294
00:14:18,200 --> 00:14:20,300
We haven't even had sex yet.

295
00:14:20,300 --> 00:14:21,500
Fair enough.

296
00:14:21,500 --> 00:14:23,600
When's your break?

297
00:14:28,160 --> 00:14:31,560
Don't you just hate when this happens?

298
00:14:36,830 --> 00:14:40,960
All right, how's this for revenge?

299
00:14:40,960 --> 00:14:43,430
A solution of hydrogen peroxide,

300
00:14:43,430 --> 00:14:47,030
and one of saturated potassium iodide.

301
00:14:47,030 --> 00:14:48,200
What's this?

302
00:14:48,200 --> 00:14:50,400
Mountain Dew.

303
00:14:52,560 --> 00:14:54,660
Ahh, refreshing.

304
00:14:54,660 --> 00:14:58,130
We're going to combine these chemicals with ordinary dish soap,

305
00:14:58,130 --> 00:15:02,630
creating a little exothermic release of oxygen.

306
00:15:14,000 --> 00:15:15,500
Foamy vengeance.

307
00:15:15,500 --> 00:15:16,730
Yes, exactly.

308
00:15:16,730 --> 00:15:18,900
This is brilliant, Sheldon.

309
00:15:18,900 --> 00:15:20,930
How are we going to deploy it in Kripke's office?

310
00:15:20,930 --> 00:15:22,800
Already taken care of.

311
00:15:22,800 --> 00:15:25,060
Observe.

312
00:15:25,060 --> 00:15:28,900
This is a live shot of Kripke's lab via a mini webcam

313
00:15:28,900 --> 00:15:31,630
I was able to install, thanks to a dollar bill

314
00:15:31,630 --> 00:15:33,930
discreetly placed in the night janitor's shirt pocket.

315
00:15:33,930 --> 00:15:35,300
At the same time,

316
00:15:35,300 --> 00:15:37,360
I also secured large quantities of these chemicals

317
00:15:37,360 --> 00:15:40,430
above the tiles in the drop ceiling.

318
00:15:40,430 --> 00:15:42,060
Oh, Sheldon,

319
00:15:42,060 --> 00:15:43,900
you remind me of a young Lex Luthor.

320
00:15:46,930 --> 00:15:49,060
You flatter me, sir.

321
00:15:49,060 --> 00:15:51,300
- Let me guess: motion sensors? - Yes.

322
00:15:51,300 --> 00:15:53,200
The reaction will be triggered

323
00:15:53,200 --> 00:15:55,300
when Kripke reaches the center of the room.

324
00:15:55,300 --> 00:15:57,500
Mwah, ha, ha.

325
00:16:00,030 --> 00:16:02,800
I gotta say, I am really impressed.

326
00:16:02,800 --> 00:16:05,660
This is truly the Sheldon Cooper way to get even.

327
00:16:11,130 --> 00:16:13,200
It may be low-tech,

328
00:16:13,200 --> 00:16:15,930
but I still maintain the whoopee cushion has comic validity.

329
00:16:17,600 --> 00:16:19,130
Here comes Kripke!

330
00:16:19,130 --> 00:16:20,830
Who is that with him?

331
00:16:20,830 --> 00:16:23,700
I believe that's the president of the university.

332
00:16:23,700 --> 00:16:25,460
And the board of directors.Abort! Abort!

333
00:16:25,460 --> 00:16:26,630
There is no "abort."

334
00:16:26,630 --> 00:16:28,200
How could you not put in an "abort?"

335
00:16:28,200 --> 00:16:30,600
I made a boo-boo, all right?

336
00:16:30,600 --> 00:16:32,860
I think the board will really appreciate

337
00:16:32,960 --> 00:16:35,400
how well we're using that NSA grant, President Seibert.

338
00:16:35,400 --> 00:16:37,660
Right here we have a micro-controlled plasma...

339
00:16:45,100 --> 00:16:48,030
Wow. Looks like the Ganges on laundry day.

340
00:16:50,900 --> 00:16:52,700
At least they don't know it was you.

341
00:16:54,200 --> 00:16:55,760
Hello, Kripke.

342
00:16:55,760 --> 00:16:58,130
This classic prank comes to you

343
00:16:58,130 --> 00:17:02,030
from the malevolent mind of Sheldon Cooper.

344
00:17:02,030 --> 00:17:05,500
If you'd like to see the look on your stupid face,

345
00:17:05,520 --> 00:17:09,000
this video is being instantly uploaded to YouTube.

346
00:17:09,000 --> 00:17:12,830
Oh, and a hat tip to Leonard Hofstadter and Raj Koothrappali

347
00:17:12,850 --> 00:17:17,630
for their support and encouragement in this enterprise.

348
00:17:20,030 --> 00:17:22,360
Well, I'm going back to India.What's your plan?

349
00:17:28,700 --> 00:17:30,330
Hey, here's your tip from table seven.

350
00:17:30,330 --> 00:17:31,660
Oh, thanks.

351
00:17:31,660 --> 00:17:34,200
Testing. Check.Check two.

352
00:17:35,360 --> 00:17:36,930
Oh, now what?

353
00:17:36,930 --> 00:17:38,660
You want me to throw him out?

354
00:17:38,660 --> 00:17:39,830
No, that's okay.

355
00:17:39,830 --> 00:17:42,300
Are you sure? He's small.

356
00:17:42,300 --> 00:17:44,830
I bet I can get a nice, tight spiral on him.

357
00:17:46,200 --> 00:17:48,160
I want to dedicate this number

358
00:17:48,160 --> 00:17:51,030
to a great gal who I've done wrong.

359
00:17:52,900 --> 00:17:56,330
Bernadette

360
00:17:56,330 --> 00:17:59,200
* I am so sorry for

361
00:17:59,200 --> 00:18:01,600
* Trying to propose to you

362
00:18:01,600 --> 00:18:03,830
* Bernadette

363
00:18:03,830 --> 00:18:06,260
* You found it creepy

364
00:18:06,260 --> 00:18:10,200
* But that's just the kind of thing I do *

365
00:18:12,360 --> 00:18:16,300
* I know now it was too soon to talk of love *

366
00:18:16,300 --> 00:18:20,560
* It was just a crazy idea that came to me in my tub *

367
00:18:20,560 --> 00:18:23,530
* But, Bernadette, give me one more chance *

368
00:18:23,530 --> 00:18:24,830
* Sweet Bernadette

369
00:18:24,830 --> 00:18:26,600
* I'll get the hang of this thing *

370
00:18:26,600 --> 00:18:28,660
* They call romance, Sweet Bernadette *

371
00:18:28,660 --> 00:18:30,800
* I dream to once again

372
00:18:30,800 --> 00:18:33,500
* Kiss your lips, Sweet Bernadette *

373
00:18:33,500 --> 00:18:35,160
* Sincerely yours

374
00:18:35,160 --> 00:18:37,400
* Howard Wolowitz

375
00:18:37,400 --> 00:18:38,800
* Bernadette...

376
00:18:38,800 --> 00:18:42,800
- Oh, I am so sorry. - Are you kidding?

377
00:18:42,800 --> 00:18:46,000
That's the most romantic thing anyone's ever done for me.

378
00:18:47,000 --> 00:18:49,660
* Bernadette!  Thank you, Cheesecake Factory!

